<section image-layout="blank" id="zj8Ahdt-sg" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="lIWf7XMgW4" vertical-align="center" colspan="37"><p role="eyebrow">Stillwater Rooms</p><h1 font-size="44pt">You don't need a spa. You need a better night.</h1><p font-size="lg">Stillwater Rooms — a wellness program built for boutique hotels of 20 to 80 keys</p><spacer size="24pt"></spacer><hr color="accent" thickness="md" width="xs"><p font-size="sm">A leaner wellness model for independent hotels</p></grid-item><grid-item id="NEzacg7S_t" padding="0" colspan="23"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/zgp6XaXoBq9lilqT1DRIY.png" saved-media-id="d07acd00vs7b8e6" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="restorative-hospitality-editorial" alt="Quiet editorial hospitality photograph inside a real-feeling boutique guest room, a staff member's hands turning down crisp white linen beside a sea-glass water carafe and folded sleep mask, soft evening window light, tactile pale wood and deep teal details, generous negative space, no text or logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="nLzUu8lCC5"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="kP2WkainsW"><h2>Your guest checked in already exhausted</h2><p>Rest has moved from a pleasant outcome to the reason for the trip.</p><grid spacing="small" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="T8_tOLJFKP" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4 align="center">56% · Rest and recharge</h4><p align="center">Now the number one stated motivation for leisure travel.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="h9e75d3AUV" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4 align="center">37% nature · 36% mental health</h4><p align="center">Both rank ahead of sightseeing or nightlife.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="4WW4pqhelv" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4 align="center">Solo time is rising</h4><p align="center">More than one in four plan a solo trip; 48% will add solo days around family travel.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Fpv9iq_8MA" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4 align="center">Hushpitality</h4><p align="center">Quietcations, digital detoxes and luxury defined by what has been removed.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>The guest who used to ask what's nearby now asks how quiet the room is.</b></p><p font-size="13pt">Source: <a href="https://www.ttgasia.com/2026/06/25/the-rise-of-hushpitality/">TTG Asia, “The rise of hushpitality”</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="HwtM-Sgg_M"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="_r5XNtR4hr" background-color="accent" vertical-align="center" colspan="22"><display size="xl">100%</display><h3>Addressable audience</h3><p>Every guest sleeps. No booking, time slot or willingness to undress is required.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="1qWQWzqbCy" vertical-align="center" colspan="38"><h2>Sleep is the only amenity every single guest uses</h2><p><b>36%</b> of American adults get insufficient rest. Sleep tourism is valued above <b>$690 billion</b>, with roughly <b>$400 billion</b> of further growth projected to 2028.</p><p>Guests can now judge the room by the levers that matter:</p><labels><label variant="outline">Light</label><label variant="outline">Temperature</label><label variant="outline">Sound</label><label variant="outline">Mattress support</label><label variant="outline">Air quality</label><label variant="outline">Screen exposure</label></labels><p role="caption">Sources: <a href="https://www.hoteltechupdate.com/trends/sleep-tourism-in-2026-what-guests-now-pay-premiums-for">Hotel Tech Update</a> · <a href="https://www.hotel-online.com/index.php/news/travelers-are-booking-vacations-just-to-sleep-better-inside-the-sleep-tourism-boom">Hotel Online</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="yw7SCHG4wY"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="0_F94T47VZ"><h2>The trap is thinking the answer is a spa</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="svuJdQzYOo" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><display size="md">$111k</display><h4>Per treatment room</h4><p>Approximate annual revenue in upper-upscale hotels. The benchmark depends on scale a 42-key property does not have.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="BcsekHz1Ft" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><display size="md">$18</display><h4>Spa revenue per occupied room</h4><p>Upper-upscale average, versus <b>$40</b> in luxury properties. Capture is thinner than the facility suggests.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="ay-1LA3wIb" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h3>Payroll carries the risk</h3><p>Major wellness operations produce strong total revenue, but payroll and the wider cost base continue to weigh on profitability.</p></grid-item></grid><aside><p>A 42-key hotel with two treatment rooms has bought a department, a rota and a licensing problem.</p></aside><p role="caption">Sources: <a href="https://www.hvs.com/article/8658-HVS-Performance-Report-Spa-Department-2019">HVS Spa Department Performance Report</a> · <a href="https://www.hotstats.com/hotel-industry-resources/minor-wellness-hotels-take-lead-across-2025-performance-metrics">HotStats</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="SmQpT2sBUR" vertical-align="center" background-color="accent"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="4h4BqwdcRp" vertical-align="center" colspan="18"><display size="lg">2025</display></grid-item><grid-item id="BsO5b5Ny77" vertical-align="center" colspan="42"><p role="eyebrow">The market turned</p><h1>In 2025, the small programs started beating the big ones</h1><p font-size="lg">Rate, room revenue and operating profit all moved in the same direction.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="qxONIy_pzN"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="tMdX845vZV"><h2>Minor wellness overtook major wellness on every line that matters</h2><graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="0" color-scheme="theme" text-size="md" config="{&quot;legend&quot;:{&quot;position&quot;:&quot;top&quot;}}" width="850pt" height="260pt"><properties><property key="metric" label="Metric ($)" type="label"></property><property key="minor" label="Minor wellness" type="data"></property><property key="major" label="Major wellness" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>ADR</cell><cell>250</cell><cell>232</cell></row><row><cell>RevPAR</cell><cell>170</cell><cell>161</cell></row><row><cell>GOPPAR</cell><cell>99</cell><cell>89</cell></row></data></graph><p><b>For the first time, all three moved in favor of smaller programs simultaneously.</b> The data covers more than 12,000 properties. A leaner cost base converted revenue to profit better than a bigger facility.</p><p role="caption">Source: <a href="https://www.hotstats.com/hotel-industry-resources/minor-wellness-hotels-take-lead-across-2025-performance-metrics">HotStats, 2025 wellness performance metrics</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="TaaIvDO-vn"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="was23rqoNg"><h2>Curation is the boutique advantage, not a consolation prize</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="xMNXskwkoq" colspan="20"><display size="md">$6.8T</display><h4>Wellness economy, 2024</h4><p>Forecast to reach <b>$9.8 trillion</b> by 2029.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="NrONR_ttg8" colspan="20"><display size="md">$894B</display><h4>Wellness tourism spend</h4><p>Global spend in 2024 alone.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="9-f0XIvp03" colspan="20"><display size="md">£15–£40</display><h4>Potential per stay</h4><p>From one well-chosen product, retail or experience partnership.</p></grid-item></grid><p>For a small property, the ancillary model is curation rather than spa capacity. You are competing with the guest's own bedroom, and winning that is cheaper than you think.</p><p role="caption">Sources: <a href="https://globalwellnessinstitute.org/press-room/press-releases/the-global-wellness-economy-hits-a-record-6-8-trillion-and-is-forecast-to-reach-9-8-trillion-by-2029/">Global Wellness Institute</a> · <a href="https://globalwellnessinstitute.org/what-is-wellness/what-is-wellness-tourism/">Wellness tourism data</a> · <a href="https://gobrandmatch.com/resources/ancillary-revenue/boutique-spa-wellness-measurement">BrandMatch</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="izmP5DR7q3" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="EatmqzDxY9" vertical-align="center" colspan="37"><p role="eyebrow">The operating model</p><h1>What Stillwater Rooms installs</h1><p font-size="lg">A better room, a fixed rhythm and a ledger that shows the return.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="3lW2G_ItfD" padding="0" colspan="23"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/s8Lvh7t7PWtVifT51tN-8.png" saved-media-id="yqn3ydqm9nhhpwn" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="restorative-hospitality-editorial" alt="Quiet editorial hospitality photograph of a boutique hotel room being prepared, staff hands placing a tea cup, analog alarm clock and folded sleep mask on a pale wood bedside table, crisp white linen and subtle sea-glass details under natural window light, no text or logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="wRI-XAFPBF" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="cZQqQsYECw"><h2>Three layers, none of which is a construction project</h2><grid marker="icon" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="center" marker-straddle="true" spacing="medium" padding-top="42pt"><grid-item id="HeDR08s03C" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/44ywiRSuIubBHWZkEQNw.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="quiet hotel bedroom moon" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A rectangular shape with a pitched roof and a small rectangle representing a door, symbolizing a place for rest and sleep.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>The room</h3><p>A specified sleep environment in every key. The physical product is delivered once.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="xh5QTZiiCc" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/2UpHp5Et1qtd9avl7uJG.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="weekly calendar local practitioner" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Seven short lines extending from a central point, forming a symmetrical pattern to signify the concept of a week.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>The rhythm</h3><p>Weekly rituals run by vetted local practitioners on revenue share, not payroll.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Zsyam7NPw1" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/Hz8dji8ViGQtRpEaaRXyph.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="hotel revenue report ledger" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black shape of a building with a triangular roof, symbolizing accommodation and hospitality, embodying the concept of a hotel.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>The ledger</h3><p>Bookable packages loaded into your booking engine, so the program appears as revenue.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>No treatment rooms. No new department. No licensed-practitioner headcount on your P&amp;L.</b></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="fXh-iBezV2"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="r1DAcxeBga"><h2>Layer one — the room, specified to six levers</h2><grid marker="icon" marker-size="small" marker-position="inline" spacing="small"><grid-item id="L5ffuKMoV_" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/XzTT2wd8WvwZA5HfY8NNuV.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="bedside lamp blackout curtain" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Two rectangular shapes connected in the middle, resembling curtains drawn together, symbolizing privacy, separation, or concealment.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Light</h4><p font-size="sm">Blackout to under 1 lux at the pillow, plus a 2200K bedside lamp that doesn't suppress melatonin.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Tdbl-0_ek1" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/xqILbkjWNByHuA6d6F6N.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="quiet room sound waves" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black line drawing of a rounded chair and a square on a wall, symbolizing a room with basic furniture.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Sound</h4><p font-size="sm">Door sweeps and a white-noise unit, targeting a measured 30 dBA at the bed.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="yCKRm1oWWf" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/44ywiRSuIubBHWZkEQNw.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="bedroom thermostat duvet" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A rectangular shape with a pitched roof and a small rectangle representing a door, symbolizing a place for rest and sleep.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Temperature</h4><p font-size="sm">18.5°C at turndown, with cooling and warming duvet weights offered at booking.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="lg7p-_0l6z" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/6KIfMEyzxievtAriWqqb.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="hotel pillow mattress topper" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A rectangular shape with two rounded edges and a flat bottom, symbolizing the concept of a pillow for resting or sleeping.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Surface</h4><p font-size="sm">Mattress topper and three pillow densities, chosen at check-in rather than requested later.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="IQ-EiS3lrN" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/0bCCs6pYivEc9l2IzTXf.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="quiet air purifier filter" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black silence symbol in a white space, conveying the concept of quietness and stillness.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Air</h4><p font-size="sm">A quiet HEPA unit per room, with filters on a logged 90-day cycle.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="NMYws6uuZY" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/resPTVcMWK8eITfnQThy.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="phone charging drawer analog clock" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A handheld device with a receiver and keypad silhouette, symbolizing communication and mobile connectivity.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Screens</h4><p font-size="sm">Charging drawer outside arm's reach and an analog alarm clock, so the phone has no excuse.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption">Every lever is measurable, so the website promise is one the hotel can audit.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="IXLSddDOB7"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="3g7enS__H_" colspan="38"><h2>Layer two — the rhythm, without the rota</h2><p>Four sessions a week, in a lobby corner at 7am, a courtyard or a roof terrace.</p><table col-widths="[24,42,34]" header-row="true" borders="rows" striped="false"><tbody><tr><td><p>Day</p></td><td><p>Fixed session</p></td><td><p>Operating rule</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Tuesday</p></td><td><p>Sunrise breathwork</p></td><td><p>Guests can plan ahead</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Wednesday</p></td><td><p>Restorative stretch</p></td><td><p>Existing hotel space</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Friday</p></td><td><p>Sunrise breathwork</p></td><td><p>Same time each week</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Sunday</p></td><td><p>Sound bath</p></td><td><p>Revenue-share delivery</p></td></tr></tbody></table></grid-item><grid-item id="9jk08f0IgH" background-color="accent" vertical-align="center" colspan="22"><display size="lg">0</display><h3>Fixed variable cost</h3><p>Stillwater sources, vets, insures and schedules contracted practitioners. They are paid from session revenue, so an empty session costs the hotel nothing.</p><p>The front desk sells it with one line at check-in and a card on the bed.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="7H6aLy4DCJ"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="LsQmtCtvWU"><h2>Layer three — the ledger, so it reads as revenue</h2><grid spacing="small"><grid-item id="nBKP4PB0T6" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="solid">Rate add-on</label></labels><h4>The Quiet Night package</h4><p>Full sleep specification, turndown ritual and a late 2pm checkout.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="uv2nIegsL7" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="outline">Bookable</label></labels><h4>Session passes</h4><p>Single-session drop-in or stay-length pass, sold at reservation or at the desk.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="WKggYUpkrQ" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="outline">Retail margin</label></labels><h4>The takeaway kit</h4><p>Sleep mask, magnesium balm, caffeine-free tea and the room's pillow spray.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="kBThFmkWyo" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="outline">Monthly</label></labels><h4>The report</h4><p>Attach rate, session fill, retail per occupied room and ADR delta between program and non-program stays.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>If it is not measured against existing KPIs, it will be cut in the first soft quarter. We measure it from week one.</b></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="j9r_21SeSq"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="1KZ8ZTjZal"><h2>Worked example: The Ardmore, 42 keys</h2><p font-size="sm"><b>Illustrative example.</b> Stillwater Rooms and The Ardmore are fictional prototype names. These figures transparently model the stated assumptions—not real-client results or a forecast of yours.</p><table col-widths="[24,52,24]" header-row="true" borders="rows" striped="false" style="--table-cell-padding: 4pt 12pt;"><tbody><tr><td><p>Line</p></td><td><p>Stated assumption</p></td><td><p>Annual result</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Occupied room-nights</p></td><td><p>42 keys, 70% annual occupancy, $265 ADR</p></td><td><p><b>10,731</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Quiet Night package</p></td><td><p>$85, at a 12% attach rate → 1,288 packages</p></td><td><p><b>$109,480</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Sessions</p></td><td><p>4 per week × 52 weeks, averaging 5 paying guests at $25</p></td><td><p><b>$26,000</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Retail</p></td><td><p>$3.50 per occupied room-night</p></td><td><p><b>$37,559</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Gross incremental revenue</b></p></td><td><p></p></td><td><p><b>$173,039</b></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Less</p></td><td><p>Practitioner share: 55% of sessions ($14,300) · Retail cost of goods: 45% ($16,902) · Program fee: $3,200/mo ($38,400)</p></td><td><p></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><b>Net contribution</b></p></td><td><p>One-time fit-out ≈ $26,000 · Payback inside four months</p></td><td><p><b>≈ $103,400</b></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p font-size="sm"><b>At an 8% attach rate, the model still clears $65,000.</b> The assumptions are yours to stress-test before signing anything.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="V4VlbrJm7T"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="utbsa2Hygw" vertical-align="center" colspan="35"><h2>It should sound like your hotel, not like a franchise</h2><p>An independent property can root its program in local traditions, ingredients and practitioners. A chain designs for repeatability across markets; specificity gives a boutique hotel pricing power and loyalty that commodity wellness cannot.</p><p><b>We source locally by default.</b> Tea from a regional producer, balm from a maker within 100 miles and practitioners from your city.</p><p><b>We write it in your voice.</b> Stillwater supplies the standards. Your team builds the names, copy and rituals in the onboarding workshop.</p><p role="caption">Source: <a href="https://hotelkonzept.com/spa-concept-development-independent-hotels/">HotelKonzept, spa concept development for independent hotels</a>.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="uNb5xmEUX9" padding="0" colspan="25"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/BH241QhIfpS9KWcJtZZHk.png" saved-media-id="a6n2y4ljzb1jei8" source="image.ai-generated" fill="true" image-style="restorative-hospitality-editorial" alt="Quiet editorial hospitality photograph of a local maker's hands preparing a small batch of caffeine-free herbal tea and botanical balm for a boutique hotel, glass jars without labels, pale wood worktable, crisp white cloth and restrained sea-glass accents under natural window light, no text or logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="hY8zT0GFpg"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="bvU_3OhO3l"><h2>What it costs and how we get paid</h2><grid spacing="small"><grid-item id="kM9Fl2PLgt" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>One-time fit-out</h4><p font-size="sm"><b>Roughly $550–$700 per key.</b> Covers the six levers, installed over a low-occupancy week. You own the hardware outright.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="SKDkqEGJZc" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>Program fee</h4><p font-size="sm">A flat monthly retainer scaled to key count, covering sourcing, practitioner management, insurance, training, collateral and reporting.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Grfa7pkV6X" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>Practitioner share</h4><p font-size="sm">Paid only from session revenue. It never touches fixed cost.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="QuV0yYcAlR" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>No lock-in</h4><p font-size="sm">A 12-month initial term, then 60 days' notice. The rooms, supplier relationships and training stay with you.</p></grid-item></grid><blockquote><p>We'd rather earn the renewal than litigate the exit.</p></blockquote></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="1JxptAZXkF" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="v6nV7rLBjM"><h2>Ninety days from signature to first Quiet Night sold</h2><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="small" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="gY4t2gq_tT" colspan="12"><h4>Days 1–14 · Audit</h4><p font-size="sm">Measure light, sound and temperature in sample rooms. Pull twelve months of ADR and occupancy.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="GvhgqPXGiN" colspan="12"><h4>Days 15–35 · Design</h4><p font-size="sm">Workshop with the GM and front-desk lead. Set naming, pricing, suppliers and practitioner shortlist.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Mzx0VpTG2H" colspan="12"><h4>Days 36–60 · Install</h4><p font-size="sm">Work floor by floor in the softest week. No room leaves inventory for more than one night.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="n-5vSPEIWs" colspan="12"><h4>Days 61–75 · Train and load</h4><p font-size="sm">Front-desk scripts, rate plan, photography and a soft launch to repeat guests.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="_2nfFjZVMg" colspan="12"><h4>Days 76–90 · Open and measure</h4><p font-size="sm">Full launch, first monthly report and first attach-rate review against baseline.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="Z5WDsb0Pkq"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="fz8CAzh3Tj"><h2>The four questions every GM asks us</h2><grid spacing="small"><grid-item id="l4qzO2RMPI" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>“What if the sessions don't fill?”</h4><p font-size="sm">They cost nothing when they do not. Practitioners are paid from session revenue. At day 90, we drop the cadence that is not working.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="QYKWm2XPBN" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>“Will this disrupt housekeeping?”</h4><p font-size="sm">The turndown ritual adds roughly 90 seconds per room and is trained in one shift. Air filters sit on a 90-day log, not a daily task.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="NKwrwi356Y" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>“Our rooms are small.”</h4><p font-size="sm">Good. Every room lever takes no floor area. Sessions use space the hotel already owns and does not currently monetize.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Fkja6-Kx8L" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>“Can we start with one floor?”</h4><p font-size="sm">Yes. Start with a 10-key pilot using the same measurement. Roll out fully or walk away at 120 days.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="J6Z2WH4q3x" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="7zbuMI7ijT" vertical-align="center" colspan="38"><p role="eyebrow">The pilot decision</p><h1 font-size="40pt">Give us one floor and ninety days</h1><ul><li><b>The ask:</b> a 10-key pilot, with the audit starting within three weeks.</li><li><b>You commit:</b> fit-out for ten rooms, one workshop morning from the GM and a front-desk briefing.</li><li><b>You get:</b> measured attach rate and a real ADR delta against your own baseline.</li><li><b>Walk away at 120 days:</b> keep the rooms, suppliers and data.</li></ul><hr color="accent" thickness="md" width="xs"><p font-size="lg">The guest is already asking for a quieter night. The only question is whether they're paying you for it.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="A9-l_VgYV3" padding="0" colspan="22"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/4N-FK0czKBCWWtK2xEMef.png" saved-media-id="177mldeccxscfwk" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="restorative-hospitality-editorial" alt="Quiet editorial hospitality photograph at dusk in a boutique guest room, an adult guest seen only through a relaxed hand placing a phone into a bedside charging drawer beside an analog clock and glass of water, crisp linen, deep teal shadow and soft sea-glass window light, observed rather than staged, no text or logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>